 ## Overview
-1D Transfer Function Generation and Sampling maps scalar volume data values to RGBA color and opacity properties using a one-dimensional lookup table (LUT) [S1, book]. It solves the problem of visualizing quantitative scalar fields by assigning optical properties based on intensity alone [S4, unofficial]. Unlike [Parametric Function Generation](5349-parametric-function-generation.md) which computes values analytically, or [2D Transfer Function](7592-2d-transfer-function.md) which incorporates gradient magnitude, this method relies on piecewise linear interpolation between user-defined control points [S5, unofficial]. It differs from [3D Color Cube Interpolation](13036-bipolar-scalar-to-color-mapping-with-level-set-support.md) by interpolating color *indices* (texture coordinates) rather than RGB values directly, ensuring all sampled colors remain within the defined colorbar and avoiding artifacts like purple hues between red and blue regions [S3, unofficial].
+1D Transfer Function Generation and Sampling maps scalar volume data values to RGBA color and opacity properties using a one-dimensional lookup table (LUT) [S1, book]. It solves the problem of quantitative data visualization by converting numerical voxel intensities into visual appearance (color and transparency) [S4, unofficial]. Unlike [Parametric Function Generation](5349-parametric-function-generation.md) which computes values analytically, or [Color Blending](5150-color-blending.md) which composites final pixels, this method performs a deterministic, per-voxel assignment based on a pre-computed 1D LUT [S1, book]. It builds on [Lookup Table Implementation](5347-lookup-table-implementation.md) by defining the specific algorithm for constructing the LUT from user-defined control points and sampling it during rendering.
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:**
-*   Scalar volume data values $v$ (typically normalized to $[0, 1]$ or mapped to integer indices).
-*   A set of control points $P = \{(x_i, c_i, \alpha_i)\}$, where $x_i$ is the scalar position, $c_i$ is the RGBA color, and $\alpha_i$ is the opacity.
-*   Optional Gaussian objects for opacity modulation [S9, unofficial].
-*   LUT resolution $N$ (typically 256, 1024, or 65536) [S2, unofficial][S9, unofficial].
+**Inputs and Outputs**
+*   **Inputs:** A set of control points defining the transfer function. Each control point consists of a scalar value (position) and an RGBA color vector. Optional inputs include Gaussian opacity objects or separate alpha control points [S9, unofficial].
+*   **Outputs:** A 1D texture (LUT) containing $N$ entries of RGBA values, typically stored as a contiguous array of 4-component vectors [S2, unofficial].
 
-**Outputs:**
-*   A 1D texture (or array) of size $N$ containing RGBA values, uploaded to GPU memory [S1, book][S2, unofficial].
-*   Sampled color $C$ and opacity $\alpha$ for each voxel value.
+**Data Structures**
+The LUT is stored as a contiguous array of size $N$ (where $N$ is a power of two, e.g., 256, 1024, 4096) [S1, book][S9, unofficial].
+*   **Format:** Typically `float4` (32-bit floats per component) or 8-bit integers (`uint8_t[4]`) [S1, book][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Layout:** A 1D array where index $i$ corresponds to the normalized value mapping to entry $i$. In some hardware implementations, the LUT may be 4096 entries deep with 36-bit entries (8-bit RGB, 12-bit alpha) [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Control Point Structure:**
+    *   `ControlPoint`: Fields `value` (scalar/int), `color` (TFColor).
+    *   `TFColor`: Fields `r`, `g`, `b`, `a` (all float, range [0.0, 1.0]) [S5, unofficial].
+    *   `AlphaControl`: Fields `pos` (vec2f) where `pos.x` is value and `pos.y` is alpha [S9, unofficial].
 
-**Data Structures:**
-*   **Control Point:** A structure containing `value` (scalar position, float), `color` (RGBA, float4), and optionally `alpha` (float) [S5, unofficial][S9, unofficial].
-*   **LUT Array:** A contiguous array of $N$ vectors, typically `float4` (RGBA) or `uint8_t[4]` [S1, book][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Texture:** A 1D or 2D texture (width $N$, height 1) with format `GL_RGBA32F` or `GL_RGBA8` [S2, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
