 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** LUT generation is $O(N \cdot K)$ where $N$ is the LUT size and $K$ is the number of control points (or $O(N \log K)$ if using binary search for interval finding) [S9, unofficial]. Sampling is $O(1)$ per voxel [S1, book].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the LUT storage [S1, book].
-*   **Performance:** Hardware interpolation using 1D textures is efficient and widely supported [S1, book]. Direct lookup is faster but lacks smoothness [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, intuitive user interface (control points), efficient GPU sampling [S1, book].
-*   **Limitations:** Cannot capture correlations between multiple scalar fields (unlike 2D TF) [S1, book]. Linear interpolation may not capture complex feature boundaries [S5, unofficial]. Color interpolation artifacts if 3D color cube method is used instead of 1D texture [S3, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Single scalar field visualization, real-time rendering, interactive exploration [S1, book].
-*   **When not to use:** Multivariate data visualization requiring correlation analysis [S1, book].
+*   **Time Complexity:** LUT construction is $O(N \cdot C)$ where $N$ is LUT size and $C$ is number of control points (due to sorting and interval search). Sampling is $O(1)$ per voxel.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the LUT storage. For 8-bit data, $N=256$; for 16-bit, $N=65536$ [S8, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Hardware interpolation using 1D textures is very fast, requiring only a single texture fetch per sample [S1, book].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, efficient, allows smooth transitions between materials [S1, book].
+*   **Limitations:** Cannot capture correlations between multiple scalar fields (requires 2D TF) [S1, book]. Linear interpolation may not capture complex feature boundaries as well as higher-order methods [S5, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Single scalar field visualization, real-time rendering, simple material classification.
+*   **When Not to Use:** Multivariate data requiring joint classification, complex non-linear mappings requiring cubic splines [S5, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The 1D transfer function was introduced as a fundamental technique in volume rendering to map scalar data to optical properties [S1, book]. It is widely used in medical imaging and scientific visualization [S6, unofficial]. The method evolved from simple color tables to interactive control point-based editors [S5, unofficial]. The distinction between interpolating color indices (1D texture) and color values (3D cube) was highlighted in Smokeview documentation to avoid artifacts [S3, unofficial].
+The 1D transfer function is the simplest and most widely used type of transfer function in volume rendering [S1, book]. It maps the range of data values to color and opacity, typically implemented with 1D texture lookup tables [S1, book]. The technique was popularized in the early days of GPU volume rendering, with examples in the NVIDIA GPU Gems book [S1, book]. It is used in various visualization tools, including Smokeview for fire dynamics [S3, unofficial], medical imaging software [S12, unofficial], and game engines like Godot [S15, unofficial].
 
